OBJECTIVES = [
    "Judge whether a new fistula is ready for use and describe safe first cannulations and the response to infiltration.",
    "Relate physical findings and treatment trends to inflow, outflow, central vein, steal, infection, and aneurysm problems.",
    "Recognize and escalate suspected catheter-related bloodstream infection and catheter dysfunction.",
    "Describe the unit-wide infection-control and blood-borne virus program, including the response to staff exposure, and teach patients to protect their access.",
]
EXTRA = [
    {"after": "physiology", "id": "maturation", "nav": "Maturation", "tag": "Physiology in depth", "h2": "Maturation, readiness, and the first cannulations",
     "html": """
<p>A new fistula is a vein being asked to behave like an artery. Once the join is made, flow through the vein rises many times over. The vein responds by widening and by thickening its wall, and the feeding artery enlarges as well. A fistula is mature when the vein is wide enough to accept two needles, close enough to the skin to be found, straight enough to offer a usable length, tough enough to seal after puncture, and carrying enough flow to supply the blood pump. Many fistulas never reach this point without help, which is why a new fistula is examined at each visit long before anyone plans to needle it.</p>
<p><strong>The &ldquo;rule of 6s.&rdquo;</strong> A widely taught rule of thumb, inherited from earlier guideline editions, describes a fistula that is likely to be usable: at about 6 weeks after creation it has a flow of about 600 mL/min, a diameter of about 6 mm, and lies no more than about 6 mm below the skin. The numbers are an aid to memory, not a test the access must pass, and flow and depth can only be measured by ultrasound. Its most practical message is the time point: a fistula that looks or feels unpromising at about 6 weeks is referred back to the surgeon or access team then, while the problem can still be corrected, and not left for months on a catheter.</p>
<p><strong>What readiness feels like.</strong> With a light tourniquet, the vein is easily seen or felt over a straight segment long enough for two needles, the wall feels firm and springy, the thrill is continuous and strongest at the join, and the vein softens when the arm is raised. The decision to begin cannulation is made by the nephrologist or surgeon together with an experienced access nurse, and is written in the record.</p>
<p><strong>First cannulations</strong> carry the highest risk of damage, because the wall is still thin. Units reduce that risk in similar ways, each set out in their own procedure:</p>
<ul>
<li>The most experienced cannulator available performs the first several attempts.</li>
<li>Smaller needles and a lower blood flow are used at first and increased stepwise over the following sessions as the access tolerates them.</li>
<li>Some units begin with one needle in the fistula for blood withdrawal and return through the existing catheter.</li>
<li>A tourniquet is used for every fistula cannulation, applied lightly.</li>
<li>The number of attempts by one nurse is limited, and a second cannulator is called early.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Infiltration</strong> means the needle tip has passed out of the vessel and blood is entering the tissue. It shows as sudden pain, swelling, or a hardening lump at the site, often with a rise in venous pressure if the venous needle is involved. The principle is to stop adding blood to the tissue: stop the pump, tell the senior nurse, and manage the needle as the unit protocol directs, taking into account whether heparin has already been given. Common practice is a cold compress on the first day to limit the bleed and warm compresses on later days to help it clear, with the access rested or needled well away from the bruise until swelling settles. A large or rapidly expanding swelling, numbness, or a cool hand is reported to the physician at once. The patient is told what happened and what to watch for at home; an unexplained painful arm destroys trust in the next cannulation.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "maturation", "id": "exam", "nav": "Examination in depth", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Physical examination in depth: finding the problem before it closes the access",
     "html": """
<p>Think of the access as a hose with a tap at one end (the artery) and a drain at the other (the veins leading to the heart). A partly closed tap gives a limp hose with weak flow. A partly blocked drain gives a tense, pounding hose. Most of the examination is deciding which of the two the access resembles, and the vascular access guideline supports regular examination by trained staff as the main way of detecting a failing access.[[va2019]]</p>
<p><strong>Inspection.</strong> Compare both arms. Look along the whole access and up to the shoulder, neck, chest wall, and face for swelling and for visible veins that were not there before. Look at the skin over every needle area for thinning, shine, loss of pigment, scabs that do not heal, and ulcers. Look at the hand for color, wounds, and wasting.</p>
<p><strong>Palpation.</strong> A healthy fistula is soft and easily compressed, with a continuous thrill that is strongest at the join and fades upward. A narrowing changes the feel in a predictable way: below it the vessel is tense and the thrill gives way to a forceful, hammering pulse; directly over it a short, localized thrill may be felt; above it the vessel is softer.</p>
<p><strong>Auscultation.</strong> The normal bruit is low-pitched and continuous through systole and diastole. Over a narrowing it becomes higher-pitched and may be heard only in systole.</p>
<p><strong>Two simple bedside tests for fistulas,</strong> carried out by staff trained in them:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Arm elevation.</em> Raise the arm above the level of the heart. A normal fistula collapses, at least partly, because blood drains freely. If the vein stays distended up to a certain point and collapses only beyond it, the outflow is narrowed at that point.</li>
<li><em>Pulse augmentation.</em> Briefly compress the fistula with one finger some distance above the join, and feel the segment between the finger and the join with the other hand. With good inflow, the pulse in that segment becomes clearly stronger. A weak or absent increase suggests a problem on the inflow side: the artery, the join, or the first part of the vein.</li>
</ul>
<p>Neither test is performed on an access whose thrill has newly disappeared; that access is not compressed, and the finding is reported at once. Elevation is not informative for grafts, which do not collapse.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Likely problem</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bleeding after needle removal that takes clearly longer than the patient&rsquo;s usual, over more than one session</td><td>Outflow narrowing raising pressure in the access; or excess anticoagulant</td><td>Report; review anticoagulant dose and stop time with the physician; request access review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Venous pressures rising from session to session at the same needle size and blood flow</td><td>Outflow narrowing</td><td>Chart the trend, not single values; report for access review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Difficult cannulation, clots drawn back, very negative arterial pressure, unable to reach ordered flow</td><td>Inflow problem or narrowing within the access</td><td>Report; do not compensate indefinitely with lower flow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swelling of the whole arm, shoulder, breast, neck, or face; new veins across the chest wall or shoulder</td><td>Narrowing of a central vein, often after earlier catheters</td><td>Report for imaging; measure and record arm circumference if unit practice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cold, pale, or bluish hand; pain or numbness, worse during dialysis; fingertip wounds</td><td>Steal: the access diverts blood away from the hand</td><td>Report promptly; rest pain, weakness, or tissue loss needs urgent surgical review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Redness, warmth, tenderness, swelling, or pus; fever</td><td>Access infection; in a graft this can involve the whole graft</td><td>Do not cannulate the area; notify the physician; cultures as ordered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aneurysm with thin, shiny, or discolored skin, a scab or ulcer, or rapid enlargement</td><td>Skin about to break down over a high-pressure vessel; risk of sudden, life-threatening bleeding</td><td>Do not cannulate there; notify the nephrologist the same session for urgent surgical referral; teach the patient and family direct pressure and how to call for help</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Findings are written so that the next nurse can compare: where on the access, what was felt and heard, and what has changed. A trend across three sessions is worth more than one alarming number, and it is the nurse at the chair who is in a position to see it.[[va2019]]</p>
"""},
    {"after": "bedside", "id": "cannulation", "nav": "Cannulation technique", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Cannulation technique: where the needles go and why",
     "html": """
<p>Every needle leaves a small hole that heals with scar. Where those holes are placed over months and years decides whether the vein stays an even tube or develops weak, ballooned segments separated by narrowings.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it is</th><th>Consequences</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Rope-ladder (site rotation)</td><td>Each session&rsquo;s needles are placed a short distance from the last, working along the full usable length and then starting again</td><td>Spreads wear evenly. The default technique for fistulas and the only acceptable one for grafts[[va2019]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area puncture (&ldquo;one-site-itis&rdquo;)</td><td>Needles repeatedly placed in the same small zones, usually because they are easy and less painful</td><td>The wall weakens and balloons into aneurysms; the overlying skin thins; narrowings form between the bulges. Not a recognized technique, but the commonest pattern in practice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buttonhole (constant site)</td><td>The identical hole, angle, and depth each time, creating a track later entered with a blunt needle after the scab is removed</td><td>Associated with a higher rate of access infection. The guideline limits it to selected situations, such as a very short usable segment or self-cannulation, with strict technique; never used in grafts[[va2019]]</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Patients often ask for &ldquo;the usual spot&rdquo; because scarred skin hurts less. Explaining, with the patient&rsquo;s own arm as the illustration, that the comfortable spot is the one being worn out is part of cannulation, and a simple site diagram in the record helps every nurse continue the rotation.</p>
<p><strong>Needle direction and spacing.</strong> The venous (return) needle always points in the direction of blood flow, toward the heart, so that returned blood is carried away. The arterial needle may point with or against the flow according to unit practice; what matters is that it sits upstream of the venous needle and far enough from the join. The two needle tips are kept well apart, because blood returned too close to the arterial needle is drawn straight back into the circuit and dialyzed again. This recirculation lowers the delivered dose without any alarm. The minimum distances are those in the unit procedure.</p>
<p><strong>Tourniquet.</strong> A light tourniquet distends and steadies a fistula for cannulation and is released once the needles are in place, as the unit procedure directs. It is not used on a graft, which does not distend and can be damaged or clotted by compression.</p>
<p><strong>Securing.</strong> Needles are taped in the unit&rsquo;s standard pattern so that a pull on the line is taken by the tape and not by the needle, the lines are looped and anchored with enough slack for the patient to move, and connections are tightened by hand and checked. The same method is used by every nurse so that a missing piece of tape is noticed. This, together with keeping the site visible, is the main defense against needle dislodgement, since machine alarms cannot be relied on to detect it.[[vnd2008]]</p>
<p><strong>Hemostasis after needle removal.</strong> The sequence matters:</p>
<ol>
<li>Withdraw the needle completely, at the angle it went in, before any pressure is applied. Pressing while the needle is still in the vessel drags the cutting edge across the wall.</li>
<li>Apply pressure with two fingers, covering both the hole in the skin and the hole in the vessel, which lies slightly further along the needle track.</li>
<li>Press firmly enough to stop bleeding but lightly enough that a thrill can still be felt above and below the fingers. Pressure that stops flow can clot the access.</li>
<li>Hold steadily for the time in the unit procedure without lifting to look, then check and dress. One needle is removed and controlled at a time unless the patient or a second person holds the other site.</li>
</ol>
<p>Mechanical clamps apply a pressure nobody can feel and are avoided where possible, particularly on new accesses and grafts; if the unit permits them, the thrill is checked while the clamp is on and the patient is not left unobserved. Dressings are never wrapped tightly around the whole arm. Bleeding that restarts, or that regularly takes much longer than that patient&rsquo;s norm, is documented and reported as an access finding and not treated as an inconvenience.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "cannulation", "id": "catheters", "nav": "Catheters in depth", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Catheters in depth: care, dysfunction, and infection",
     "html": """
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Non-tunneled catheter</th><th>Tunneled cuffed catheter</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Design</td><td>Enters the vein directly at the skin puncture</td><td>Runs under the skin for several centimeters before entering the vein; a fabric cuff in the tunnel becomes anchored by tissue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intended use</td><td>Short-term, usually for inpatients starting dialysis urgently</td><td>Weeks to months, as a bridge to a fistula or graft, or longer when no other access is possible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main risks</td><td>Infection rises quickly with time; dislodgement; bleeding</td><td>Bloodstream infection, clot and fibrin sheath, central vein narrowing</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Site.</strong> The right internal jugular vein is preferred because it offers the straightest path to the heart. The subclavian vein is avoided: catheters there are strongly associated with narrowing of the vein, and a narrowed subclavian vein can make every future fistula or graft in that arm unusable.[[va2019]] Femoral catheters restrict walking, are generally short-term, and are watched closely for infection.</p>
<p><strong>Exit-site care.</strong> At each session, with nurse and patient masked, the old dressing is removed and the site inspected for redness, crusting, drainage, tenderness along the tunnel, and any visible cuff. The skin is cleaned with the unit&rsquo;s antiseptic, allowed to dry, and covered with a sterile dressing; the unit policy states whether an antimicrobial ointment or impregnated dressing is applied. A cuff that has become visible, a catheter that has moved outward, or a missing suture on a non-tunneled catheter is reported before the catheter is used.</p>
<p><strong>Hubs, caps, and locks.</strong> Organisms reach the blood mainly through the inside of the catheter, carried in on hubs and connectors, which is why hub disinfection matters more than anything done to the skin. Between sessions each limb is filled with a locking solution, such as heparin or citrate, chosen by order. The lock is withdrawn and discarded before use, as the procedure directs, and at the end it is instilled to exactly the volume printed on each limb; overfilling pushes anticoagulant into the patient. The catheter is used for dialysis only. It is not used for blood sampling, infusions, or medicines by other teams except in an emergency or on the nephrologist&rsquo;s order, and patients are taught to say so when admitted elsewhere.</p>
<p><strong>Poor flow.</strong> Start with what is outside the patient: clamps, kinks under the dressing, and the patient&rsquo;s position, since turning the head, coughing, or lying flatter can move the tip away from the vein wall. If blood cannot be withdrawn easily, the limb is not flushed forcefully, because that can dislodge clot. Reversing the limbs raises recirculation and lowers the delivered dose; it is done only if unit policy allows, as a temporary measure, and is documented and reported. Persistent poor flow is reported so that the physician can order a thrombolytic lock, imaging, or catheter exchange.</p>
<p><strong>Recognizing catheter-related BSI.</strong> The classic story is fever or shaking chills beginning during dialysis or soon after, as organisms growing inside the catheter are flushed into the circulation. Older and diabetic patients may show only low blood pressure, confusion, vomiting, or feeling unwell. The exit site often looks normal. When a catheter patient has fever or chills:</p>
<ul>
<li>Take a full set of vital signs and assess the exit site and tunnel.</li>
<li>Notify the physician during the session. Chills in a patient with a catheter are treated as a possible bloodstream infection until shown otherwise; they are not attributed to the air conditioning.</li>
<li>Draw blood cultures as ordered <em>before</em> the first antibiotic dose, from the sites the unit protocol specifies.</li>
<li>Do not send the patient home without a medical decision. Give clear instructions on what should bring the patient back immediately.</li>
<li>In the following weeks, report new back pain, a new heart murmur, joint swelling, or persistent fever, which can indicate that infection has seeded the spine, heart valves, or joints.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Catheter reduction is a unit goal.</strong> Lowering the number of patients who dialyze through catheters was one of the core practices in the CDC collaborative, and the access guideline asks that each catheter have a plan for what replaces it.[[patel2013]][[va2019]] In practice the nurse keeps the question alive: which patients have a catheter, whether a fistula or graft has been planned, who is waiting for surgery, and what is delaying it, which in the Philippines is often the cost of the operation.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "catheters", "id": "program", "nav": "Unit infection control", "tag": "Evidence", "h2": "The unit infection-control program",
     "html": """
<p>A dialysis unit is unusual among outpatient settings: many patients, in one room, with open access to the bloodstream, cared for by staff who move between stations for hours. Blood contaminates surfaces that look clean. For this reason dialysis-specific precautions go further than standard precautions.[[cdc2001]]</p>
<p><strong>Hand hygiene and gloves.</strong> Hands are cleaned before touching a patient or anything at the station, before every aseptic task (cannulation, catheter connection, injection into the circuit), after contact with blood or body fluid, after touching the patient, after touching the machine or other surfaces at the station, and after removing gloves. Gloves are worn whenever the patient or the equipment at the station is touched. They are removed, and hands cleaned, before moving to another patient or station and when moving from a dirty task to a clean one at the same patient, for example from silencing a machine alarm to handling a catheter hub. Gloves that have touched one station never touch a shared keyboard, chart, telephone, or supply cart.[[cdc2001]]</p>
<p><strong>Clean and dirty zones.</strong> Each station is treated as contaminated. Supplies are brought to the station for one patient; unused items are discarded, kept only for that patient, or disinfected before being returned to a clean area. Medicines are drawn up in a designated clean area away from stations and carried to one patient at a time; common carts are not moved from station to station to deliver medicines or supplies, and medicines are not carried in pockets. Blood samples and used items travel to a separate dirty area.[[cdc2001]]</p>
<p><strong>Vials.</strong> Single-dose vials are used for one patient once, and leftover contents are not pooled. A multi-dose vial, where one must be used, is kept and entered only in the clean medicine area, with a new needle and new syringe for every entry, and is dated and discarded as policy requires. It is never taken to a station.[[cdc2001]]</p>
<p><strong>Machine and environment.</strong> External pressure-line protectors are changed if they become wet and are not reused; a wet protector means blood may have reached the machine&rsquo;s internal sensor, and the technician is told. After each treatment, once the patient has left, all surfaces of the chair and machine, including the front panel, clamps, and waste container, are cleaned and then disinfected for the contact time on the product label. Visible blood is removed first, because disinfectant does not work through it.[[cdc2001]]</p>
<p><strong>Audit, feedback, and patients.</strong> The units in the CDC collaborative did not rely on policy alone: they counted infections, observed hand hygiene and access care, fed the results back to staff, and taught patients about access care and signs of infection.[[patel2013]] A patient who has been told that everyone should clean their hands before touching the access is an extra observer at every session, and staff should welcome the reminder.</p>
<p><strong>Blood-borne virus program.</strong> Isolation rules were given earlier in this module. The surveillance that makes them work is set out in the CDC recommendations and adapted locally:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>CDC recommendation</th><th>Nursing role</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Testing on entry</td><td>HBV markers and HCV antibody for every patient before or at the first treatment[[cdc2001]]</td><td>Confirm results are on file and the station assignment matches them; a patient with unknown status is managed as the unit policy directs until results are available</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hepatitis B vaccination</td><td>All susceptible patients and staff; antibody response checked after the series; in patients, antibody level rechecked yearly and a booster given if it has fallen below the protective level[[cdc2001]]</td><td>Track due dates; the dialysis dose and schedule differ from the usual adult regimen and follow the product and the order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Routine retesting</td><td>HBsAg monthly in susceptible patients; HCV antibody every 6 months in previously negative patients[[cdc2001]][[hcv2022]]</td><td>Follow the unit schedule, which reflects PSN and DOH requirements; chase missing results[[psn2024]][[doh2012]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>Travel and transfers</td><td>Current serology accompanies the patient</td><td>Check results before a visiting patient is assigned a machine</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) status does not change machine assignment; standard dialysis precautions apply, and testing follows unit policy and Philippine consent requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Seroconversion</strong> means a patient who was negative becomes positive for HBV or HCV. In a dialysis unit it is treated as a sentinel event, because transmission within the unit must be assumed until excluded. The nurse who sees the result notifies the nephrologist and the infection-control officer the same day; the unit then reviews practices, machine and station history, and the results of patients who shared the shift.[[cdc2001]][[hcv2022]]</p>
<p><strong>Staff exposure.</strong> After a needlestick or a splash of blood to the eyes, mouth, or broken skin: wash the wound with soap and water or flush the mucous membrane with water, without squeezing or applying caustic agents; tell the charge nurse immediately; and go through the hospital&rsquo;s post-exposure pathway the same shift, because protective treatment is time-sensitive. The source patient&rsquo;s status is checked through that pathway, not informally. Reporting protects the nurse&rsquo;s health and entitlements and shows the unit where its sharps handling is failing.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "program", "id": "selfcare", "nav": "Patient teaching", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Teaching the patient to protect the access",
     "html": """
<p>The patient has the access for 168 hours a week and the unit sees it for about 12. Teaching is given in the patient&rsquo;s language, shown on the patient&rsquo;s own arm, repeated to a family member, and checked by asking the patient to demonstrate.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Fistula or graft</th><th>Catheter</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Feel for the thrill every morning and before sleep. If it is weaker, different, or absent, call the unit the same day; do not wait for the next session</td><td>Keep the dressing clean, dry, and intact. If it becomes wet, loose, or soiled, contact the unit; do not replace it with improvised materials unless taught how</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wash the access arm with soap and water before each session and keep the skin clean and unbroken; do not scratch or pick at scabs</td><td>Never open the caps or clamps, and never allow anyone outside the dialysis team to use the catheter</td></tr>
<tr><td>No blood pressure cuffs, blood draws, or intravenous lines in the access arm; say so to every health worker</td><td>No swimming or soaking; bathe in the way the unit teaches so that the exit site stays dry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nothing tight on the arm: watches, bracelets, elastic sleeves, bag straps. Do not carry heavy loads hanging from that forearm or sleep with the arm under the head or body</td><td>Avoid pulling on the catheter; secure it under clothing; keep scissors and razors away from it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Report redness, swelling, warmth, pain, pus, a lump that is growing, skin that looks thin or shiny, a scab that will not heal, or a cold, numb, or painful hand</td><td>Report fever, chills, pain or redness at the exit site or along the tunnel, drainage, a visible cuff, or a catheter that seems longer than before</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Bleeding at home.</strong> Every patient and household is taught this before it is needed. If a needle site bleeds after leaving the unit, press directly on the spot with clean gauze or cloth and two fingers, raise the arm, and hold without looking for the time taught by the unit; if it has not stopped, keep pressing and go to the nearest emergency department. Bleeding from a scab or ulcer over an aneurysm is an emergency from the first second: press hard directly on the bleeding point with fingers or the heel of the hand, shout for help, and get to a hospital while pressure is maintained. Towels laid over the arm soak up blood without stopping it, and a tight band around the arm above the site is not a substitute for direct pressure.</p>
<p><strong>If a catheter cap comes off or the catheter is cut or pulled out,</strong> the patient closes the clamp on that limb if it can be reached, or presses firmly on the exit site if the catheter has come out, and goes to the hospital immediately.</p>
<p>Document what was taught, to whom, and what the patient was able to demonstrate. Repeat the teaching after any access complication, when the access type changes, and at intervals, since most people remember only part of what they are told at the start of dialysis.</p>
"""},
]
